```c
if (chcr & check_bits) {
    mask |= USB_DMACHCR_DE | check_bits;
}
usb_dmac_isr_transfer_end(chan);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the driver checks for certain bits in the `CHCR` register (specifically `check_bits`) but does not ensure that the `CHCR.DE` bit is set before calling `usb_dmac_isr_transfer_end()`. This can lead to the function being called with a NULL descriptor when `CHCR.NULLE` is set, resulting in a potential null pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (chcr & USB_DMACHCR_DE)
    xfer_end = true;
if (xfer_end)
    usb_dmac_isr_transfer_end(chan);
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a check for the `CHCR.DE` bit before calling `usb_dmac_isr_transfer_end()`. By setting the `xfer_end` flag only if `CHCR.DE` is set, the function is only called when it is safe to do so, preventing the possibility of dereferencing a NULL descriptor.

